# Docs-to-Tasks Planning Rules

When asked to create tasks from documentation:

- Do not implement code.
- Create or update planning/task files only.
- Infer phases from documentation.
- Split large phases into small implementable tasks.
- Preserve dependencies.
- Mark unclear items as "Needs clarification".
- Do not invent missing architecture.

## Recommended Task Folder

If no task folder exists, create:

```text
/tasks/
  00-overview/
  01-foundation/
  02-core-features/
  03-integrations/
  04-data-and-storage/
  05-ui/
  06-testing-and-quality/
  07-advanced-later/
```

Adjust to project context if existing conventions differ.

## Task File Structure

Each task file should use:

```md
# Phase XX — Task Name

## Goal

What this task accomplishes.

## Context

Relevant context from documentation or current code.

## Scope

### Must implement

- Item
- Item

### Must not implement

- Future feature
- Unrelated work

## Inputs / Dependencies

- Prior tasks
- Required docs
- Existing modules

## Expected Outputs

- Files/modules/APIs/jobs/UI expected

## Data / Schema Requirements

Only if relevant.

## API Requirements

Only if relevant.

## Background Jobs

Only if relevant.

## UI Requirements

Only if relevant.

## Validation and Error Handling

- Expected validation
- Expected error states

## Tests Required

- Unit tests
- Integration tests
- API tests
- UI tests
- Security tests if applicable

## Definition of Done

- [ ] Feature works
- [ ] Tests added/updated
- [ ] Tests pass
- [ ] Docs updated if needed
- [ ] No unrelated features added
- [ ] Errors handled

## Notes / Clarifications Needed

- List unclear items
```

## Task Quality Rules

A task must be:

- small enough to implement independently
- testable
- scoped
- ordered
- dependency-aware
- clear about non-goals
- free from fake data or fake implementation assumptions

If too large, split it.
